<description>
Crop the 4-connected foreground sprite whose colored pattern occurs only once among otherwise duplicated sprites.

Input structure
- The background is the dominant color.
- Foreground pixels form several separated 4-connected sprites.
- Every distractor sprite has an identical colored bounding-box crop elsewhere in the grid.

Rule
- Crop each 4-connected foreground sprite to its bounding box.
- Compare the exact colored crops, including background pixels inside each crop.
- Return the only crop whose exact pattern occurs once.

Key insight
- The answer is the outlier sprite by repetition count, not by position, color id, or size alone.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "4-connected foreground sprites on the background; all distractor sprite crops repeat elsewhere",
  "transform": "crop and return the single sprite whose exact colored bounding-box crop occurs once",
  "grid_shape": "bounding-box crop of the singleton sprite"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 11-13 rows x 11-13 cols, background 0
  objects:
    count: 5 (exactly), as 4-connected foreground components
    groups: 3 distinct crop signatures — 2 repeated pairs + 1 unique singleton
    bbox_crop: 1-5 rows x 1-3 cols, area 3-6 fg pixels per sprite
    colors_per_sprite: 1-2 distinct non-bg colors
    color_overlap: colors MAY be shared across groups (not required to be disjoint)
    bbox_isolation: no two sprite bounding boxes overlap; each crop contains only its own fg pixels
    placement: scattered across grid; sprites may touch grid border [observed]
  output: the bounding-box crop of the unique (non-duplicated) sprite

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct construction (task is simple enough for forward generation)
  steps:
    1. Pick grid size H, W in [11, 13].
    2. Create 3 distinct small sprite crops (1-5 rows x 1-3 cols each, area 3-6).
       Use 3-4 distinct fg colors total; colors may be shared across sprites.
       Ensure all three crop signatures (including bg cells within bbox) are unique.
    3. Duplicate sprite A to get 2 copies; duplicate sprite B to get 2 copies;
       keep sprite C as the singleton (5 sprites total).
    4. Place all 5 sprites on the grid so no two bounding boxes overlap
       and each is at least 1 cell from any other bbox.
       Allow touching grid border.
    5. Output is the crop of sprite C.
```
</generation_spec>
